From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: psigod <psigod@example.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 15:43:51 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: hci_qca: Start with both sides asleep after a
 power off

qca_hci_shutdown() stops the IBS timers, so a controller powered off
shortly after a command keeps the host's TX state awake. After the next
power on, the host then sends commands without waking the controller,
which does not answer them, and the HCI init fails.

Reset both IBS states to asleep in qca_power_off(), as qca_open() does.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
index bd9f68bdb..fedf5f212 100644
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
@@ -2243,6 +2243,15 @@ static void qca_power_off(struct hci_uart *hu)
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&qca->hci_ibs_lock, flags);
 	set_bit(QCA_IBS_DISABLED, &qca->flags);
 	qca_flush(hu);
+
+	/* A controller that is powered on again starts with both sides
+	 * asleep, as on the first open: a stale awake state would send it
+	 * commands without waking it first.
+	 */
+	timer_delete(&qca->wake_retrans_timer);
+	timer_delete(&qca->tx_idle_timer);
+	qca->tx_ibs_state = HCI_IBS_TX_ASLEEP;
+	qca->rx_ibs_state = HCI_IBS_RX_ASLEEP;
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&qca->hci_ibs_lock, flags);
 
 	/* Non-serdev device usually is powered by external power
